PART I — THE 24-STAGE ROSTER

2. Completely redesign Stage Selection

The current Stage Selection screen has a large live preview on the left and two vertically stacked stage cards on the right.

This design cannot scale to a large number of stages.

Replace the two-card presentation with a proper 24-slot stage roster.

Layout

Create a compact, modern grid that accommodates 24 stage positions.

Suggested layout:

* A six-column by four-row grid on sufficiently large screens, when space allows.
* Adapt the column count and tile dimensions responsively.
* On compact landscape devices, preserve readable and usable cards within a scrollable roster.
* On tall or narrow displays, allow the roster and preview to stack.
* Never allow stage tiles or controls to overflow their containers.

The existing large live stage preview should remain a prominent part of the design.

The screen should feel like a professional fighting game’s stage-selection interface, rather than a simple collection of large buttons.

24 stage positions

There must be exactly 24 reserved stage positions.

Initially:

Slot	Stage	Status
01	Desert	Available
02	City	Available
03	Asperitas	Available
04–24	Future stages	Unavailable placeholders

The remaining 21 positions must be visually recognizable as empty or upcoming stage slots.

They should have subdued styling, such as a subtle lock symbol or placeholder graphic.

Unavailable slots must not be selectable, and they must never be included in random stage selection.

Do not add 21 fake playable stages to the MAPS registry just to fill the roster.

Instead, generate the roster from the actual stage definitions, then fill the unused positions with presentation-only placeholders.

This design should allow future developers to add stages to the registry without rebuilding the selection interface.

Stage tiles

Each available stage tile should include:

* A small stage thumbnail.
* Its stage name.
* A clearly visible selected state.
* A hover/focus state.
* An indication that the tile is available.

Maintain Alva’s existing UI identity, including its dark panels, typography, borders, selected states, and green accent language.

Tiles must remain legible even when compact.

The selected tile should be visibly distinguishable from hovered or focused tiles.

Live preview

Retain the large hero preview.

When highlighting a stage, display its corresponding environment using the existing StagePreview and registered theme.

Asperitas must display its actual cloud environment.

Avoid generating unnecessarily expensive live animations for all 24 roster positions simultaneously.

The small thumbnails may be static or lightly updated, while the large preview remains animated.

The stage name and tagline should remain available within the selection experience.

Stage count

Display useful information such as:

3 available / 24 slots

Do not hardcode the available count.

It should automatically reflect changes to MAPS.

The maximum slot count should remain 24 unless intentionally expanded in the future.

