PART VI — MOVING PLATFORM PHYSICS

14. Extend Alva’s platform system correctly

The existing StageCollision class converts the platform definitions into one-way collision surfaces, while stepBody() resolves landing and grounding.

Currently, stage platforms are stationary.

This update introduces genuinely dynamic platforms.

Implement the necessary shared support cleanly.

Required functionality

Dynamic platforms must have:

* A stable identifier.
* A current position.
* A previous position.
* Their per-step displacement.
* A visible/active collision state.
* Movement boundaries.
* Their own behavior controller when needed.

Keep the original map definition separate from live simulation state.

Do not mutate the shared MAPS configuration globally during a match.

Two matches must not accidentally share the same moving-platform state.

Fighter support and movement

When a platform moves and a fighter is grounded on it, apply the appropriate platform displacement before resolving the fighter’s normal movement.

The fighter should remain supported while the platform moves.

Movement must account for both horizontal and vertical displacement.

Avoid accidental double application of displacement, grounded-state flickering, or repeated landing events.

Preserve natural jumping from the platform.

When the fighter jumps away, the platform must not continue dragging the fighter through the air.

If a platform disappears, becomes non-collidable, or moves out from under a fighter, grounding must be cleared correctly.

Collision integration

Review and update all relevant systems:

* StageCollision
* stepBody()
* Platform landing checks
* surfaceBelow()
* supportsAt()
* Fighter grounding
* Platform references stored on fighter bodies
* Relevant camera and shadow calculations

Ensure inactive cloud platforms are ignored by collision and support queries.

Any moving platform must use its current simulation position in collision calculations.

The physics and visual systems must not maintain unrelated copies of platform positions that can drift apart.

Timing

Advance dynamic platform states before fighter physics on each fixed simulation step.

Ensure the correct order for platform displacement, grounded rider movement, and collision resolution.

Rendering should use the simulation’s actual platform positions, with suitable interpolation when necessary.

Do not implement gameplay movement using CSS animations or independent requestAnimationFrame() position updates.

